macpro-fand (1.0.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixes from a fourth external review of 1.0.4 (1.0.4 was not released).
  * The budget's feasibility is judged on every loop with the CPU's present
    charge, not once at start: an allocation that stops fitting (RAPL no
    longer enforcing, PL2 not held) raises an alarm -- every fan at its cap
    -- for as long as it lasts, while the power worker only lowers limits;
    the state log reports "feasible" separately from "confirmed".
  * STATIC_SAFE limits must be whole watts too.
  * Tests: the --check-config subprocess runs on the inventory fixture.

 -- Exabit Networks <packages@exabit.io>  Tue, 06 Oct 2026 07:25:18 +0000

macpro-fand (1.0.4) unstable; urgency=high

  * Fixes from a third external review of 1.0.3 (1.0.3 was not released).
  * A power limit whose value cannot be read is first set to 10 W, the
    lowest value ever written, instead of its configured floor: with raised
    floors and older caps (or a CPU pin) below them, the floor could be an
    increase made before headroom existed.
  * An allocation that does not fit the budget only lowers limits, in normal
    operation and in the stop hook (a static-safe plan that cannot fit no
    longer raises a low CPU pin); the stop then reports incomplete recovery.
  * Every power-limit reading carries the time it was actually read: an old
    PL2 reading is no longer made fresh by a slow transaction.
  * On stop, every fan goes to full speed before the wait for the power
    worker.
  * A binary override file is ignored instead of restarting the daemon;
    PERIPH keys must be driver names; power limits in the configuration
    must be whole watts.
  * Tests: the host inventory is a fixture (both suites pass on a builder
    with no GPU), crashes count as named failures, and mutation_check.py
    requires a passing baseline and the failing check of each defect's own
    finding.

 -- Exabit Networks <packages@exabit.io>  Tue, 06 Oct 2026 06:15:43 +0000

macpro-fand (1.0.3) unstable; urgency=high

  * Fixes from a second external review of 1.0.2 (1.0.2 was not released).
  * Power transactions: a limit whose value cannot be read goes to its floor
    before anything is raised; nothing goes up until every limit is
    confirmed. A limit counts as set only at or up to 1 W below its target,
    never above; limits are written in whole watts rounded down, never below
    10 W (amdgpu takes 0 as "restore the default").
  * The CPU's charge for an unheld PL2 or RAPL not enforcing applies to
    override pins, the reset after a pin and the stop hook; an unheld PL2 is
    read every cycle and a stale reading counts as 413 W.
  * Stop: the power worker starts nothing more and the operation in progress
    is waited for before the static-safe limits are written; with an
    operation stuck in the driver no competing transaction is started (fans
    at their caps, exit status 1). No bounded operation starts after its
    deadline.
  * The stop hook takes the owner lock and leaves another running
    macpro-fand alone; it recovers with the built-in defaults whatever the
    configuration file holds (no parse or validation error can stop it).
  * Configuration: GPU_CAP floor <= init, STATIC_SAFE no lower than the
    floors, PL1 floor >= 10 W; unhashable or oversized values are errors.
  * GPU read-failure alarms persist until a good reading; GPU filters use
    the time between samples. SMC key lookups that failed are retried.
    GPU dies that are not Vega 20 are logged as unsupported.
  * Lock files are checked against the running user: both test suites run
    as an ordinary user (Rules-Requires-Root: no).

 -- Exabit Networks <packages@exabit.io>  Tue, 06 Oct 2026 04:32:09 +0000

macpro-fand (1.0.2) unstable; urgency=high

  * Power limits: one worker thread owns every power-limit read and write,
    so a blocked amdgpu call never stalls the fan loop. Each change is a
    transaction (confirmed decreases, then unreadable limits, then
    increases), every write is read back, and the first failure stops the
    transaction: nothing is raised on budget that was not released. The
    limits are re-read every 2 s and restored or retried until they read
    back; a stuck or unconfirmed limit is an alarm.
  * The stop hook writes the static-safe limits by the same rule, every
    read and write bounded, 10 s for the whole.
  * Accounting: a die without a manageable power cap is charged 200 W, in
    the budget and in the static-safe limits; the CPU is charged at PL2 as
    read back when PL2 cannot be held at PL1, and at 413 W while RAPL does
    not enforce.
  * An override pin that ends during the 60 s backstop hold waits for it.
    Without a measured DC reading for 15 s the allocation is held.
  * Configuration: the whole file is validated (types, shapes, ranges,
    finite numbers, floors that fit the budget) before anything is used; an
    invalid file is reported and the daemon exits 78 with nothing applied.
    New option --check-config. A lower GPU_CAP maximum also bounds the
    start allocation and the static-safe caps.
  * Locks: /run/macpro-fand/owner.lock makes the process that owns the
    hardware exclusive (a second instance refuses); the SMC lock moves to
    /run/macpro-fand/smc.lock, root-only (0600), never opened through a
    symlink, and waited for with a bound.
  * GPU readings carry their own read time per die. A die at its
    emergency temperature alarms even when its power cannot be read. A
    failed RAPL read no longer crashes the loop, and a wrapped energy
    counter is unwrapped. Non-finite sensor and override values are
    rejected.
  * Cleanup covers every hardware write from the first one; --dry-run
    loads no kernel module; contradictory modes are refused.
  * New failure-path test suite (tests/test_safety.py), run at build time.
  * Documentation: the override example pins 110 W, within the installed
    125 W ceiling (a pin above the configured maximum is lowered to it).

 -- Exabit Networks <packages@exabit.io>  Tue, 06 Oct 2026 03:29:00 +0000

macpro-fand (1.0.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The installed /etc/macpro-fand.conf sets GPU_CAP = {"max": 125}: GPU
    dies at most 125 W by default. The built-in range is unchanged
    (85-200 W); comment the line out for the full range.
  * The budget's reallocation clamps every die to the configured GPU_CAP
    maximum itself (a ceiling below the default share + 10 W could be
    exceeded until the working-temperature ceilings caught up).

